msgid "Fast JAR (default)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The fast JAR is the default packaging type produced by `./mvnw package` or `./gradlew build`.\n"
"It generates a `quarkus-app/` directory under your build output that contains the application JAR, an index of dependencies, and the dependency JARs themselves."
msgstr ""

msgid "Run the application with:"
msgstr "アプリケーションを実行します。"

msgid ""
"You must deploy the entire `quarkus-app/` directory.\n"
"If any file is missing, the application will not start or might not function correctly."
msgstr ""

msgid "How it works"
msgstr "動作原理"

msgid ""
"Unlike a traditional flat classpath JAR, the fast JAR uses an index that maps classes to their containing dependency JAR.\n"
"This avoids scanning every JAR on the classpath when loading a class, which results in faster startup compared to a legacy classpath JAR."
msgstr ""

msgid "When to use it"
msgstr ""

msgid "The fast JAR is the right default for most applications:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Simplest build workflow. No extra flags, extensions, or JDK requirements."
msgstr ""

msgid "Full JVM capabilities. JIT compilation delivers the best peak throughput for long-running services."
msgstr ""

msgid "Standard debugging and profiling. All JVM tooling works out of the box."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The main limitation is that the output is a directory, not a single file.\n"
"If your deployment pipeline requires a single artifact, consider the <<uber-jar,uber-JAR>> instead."
msgstr ""

msgid "Reducing JAR size"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can enable experimental tree-shaking to remove unreachable classes from runtime dependencies.\n"
"See xref:jar-tree-shaking.adoc[Tree-shaking JAR dependencies] for details."
msgstr ""

msgid "Uber-JAR"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An uber-JAR bundles the application and all its dependencies into a single JAR file.\n"
"Enable it by setting the following property in `application.properties`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The resulting JAR is placed in the build output directory with a `-runner` suffix by default.\n"
"Run it with:"
msgstr ""

msgid "An uber-JAR is convenient when:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Your deployment target expects a single file (for example, a legacy application server, a simple deployment script, or a `java -jar` invocation with no additional classpath setup)."
msgstr ""

msgid "You want to copy a single artifact to a remote server without worrying about directory structure."
msgstr ""

msgid "The trade-offs compared to the fast JAR are:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Flat structure. The uber-JAR merges all dependencies into a single archive, losing the original JAR boundaries. This can cause issues with resources that have the same path across multiple dependencies. JAR signatures from signed dependencies are also lost in the process."
msgstr ""

msgid "No container image layering. With the fast JAR, container image builders like Jib can cache dependency JARs in a separate layer so that only the thin application layer changes on rebuild. An uber-JAR is a single file, so the entire image layer must be rebuilt and pushed on every change."
msgstr ""

msgid "Customizing the uber-JAR"
msgstr ""

msgid "You can configure the uber-JAR output with the following properties:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus.package.jar.runner-suffix`: changes the file name suffix (default: `-runner`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus.package.jar.add-runner-suffix`: set to `false` to omit the suffix entirely."
msgstr ""

msgid "`quarkus.package.ignored-entries`: a comma-separated list of entries to exclude from the JAR."
msgstr ""

msgid "Signature files from dependencies are excluded by default."
msgstr ""

msgid "For full details, see the xref:maven-tooling.adoc#uber-jar-maven[uber-JAR section] of the Maven tooling guide or the xref:gradle-tooling.adoc[Gradle tooling guide]."
msgstr ""

